question.:question/worked-solution:
Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. The term involving only $z$ differentiates to zero. Use the constant multiple and power rules.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=- y^{3}\cdot 1+0\\&=- y^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
C&=1,\quad r=0\\
u&=y^{3},\quad v=\cos{\left(x y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
u_y&=3 y^{2}\\
v_y&=- x \sin{\left(x y \right)}\\
r_y&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply both terms of the product rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\
&=1\left[\left(3 y^{2}\right)\left(\cos{\left(x y \right)}\right)+\left(y^{3}\right)\left(- x \sin{\left(x y \right)}\right)\right]+\left(0\right)\\
&=- x y^{3} \sin{\left(x y \right)} + 3 y^{2} \cos{\left(x y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

