Use the **product rule**: $\nabla(fg)=g\nabla f+f\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\nabla(fg)&=(- x + 2 y)(-\mathbf i-\mathbf j) \\ &\quad+(- x - y)(-\mathbf i+2\mathbf j)
\end{aligned}
$$

The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(x,y)&=- 2 x - 2 \\ f_y(x,y)&=2 y \\ \nabla f(1,2)&=\left(-4\right)\,i+\left(4\right)\,j \\ \|\nabla f(1,2)\|&=\sqrt{\left(-4\right)^2+\left(4\right)^2} \\ &=4 \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

This gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:

$$
\begin{aligned}\mathbf n&=\frac{\nabla f(1,2)}{\|\nabla f(1,2)\|} \\ &=\frac{\left(-4\right)\,i+\left(4\right)\,j}{4 \sqrt{2}} \\ &=\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\,i+\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\,j
\end{aligned}
$$

Its negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.

Bring the variable terms together and set

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F(x,y,z)&=\left(y + 2\right)^{2}\\
&{}-\left(- x - 1\right)\\
&{}+2 \left(z + 2\right)^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The surface is the level surface $F=0$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(x,y,z)&=1
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(x,y,z)&=2 \left(y + 2\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(x,y,z)&=4 \left(z + 2\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
\left(y + 2\right)&=1\\
\left(z + 2\right)&=-1\\
\left(- x - 1\right)&=3
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these values into the partial derivatives:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(P)&=1\\
&=1
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(P)&=2 \left(1\right)\\
&=2
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(P)&=4 \left(-1\right)\\
&=-4
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\nabla F(-4,-1,-3)=\langle 1,2,-4\rangle\ne\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.

The point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:

$$
\mathbf r=\langle -4,-1,-3\rangle+t\,\langle 1,2,-4\rangle,\qquad t\in\mathbb R.
$$

