question.:question/worked-solution:
The fundamental theorem for line integrals gives the terminal potential minus the initial potential. The endpoints are

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r(0)&=\left\langle 0,3\right\rangle, \\ \mathbf r(4)&=\left\langle 2,7\right\rangle.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these points into the supplied potential:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(0,3)&=- \left(0\right)^{2} \left(3\right)^{2} - \left(0\right) - \left(3\right) \\ &=-3, \\ f(2,7)&=- \left(2\right)^{2} \left(7\right)^{2} - \left(2\right) - \left(7\right) \\ &=-205.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C\nabla f\cdot\mathrm d\mathbf r&=f(2,7)-f(0,3) \\ &=\left(-205\right)-\left(-3\right) \\ &=-202.
\end{aligned}
$$

Compute the cross product using a determinant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{p}\times \mathbf{q}&=\begin{vmatrix}\mathbf{i}&\mathbf{j}&\mathbf{k}\\2 & 4 & -3\\0 & -3 & 2\end{vmatrix}\\&=((4)\cdot (2)-(-3)\cdot (-3))\mathbf{i}\\&\quad-((2)\cdot (2)-(-3)\cdot (0))\mathbf{j}\\&\quad+((2)\cdot (-3)-(4)\cdot (0))\mathbf{k}\\&=\langle -1,-4,-6\rangle.
\end{aligned}
$$

The magnitude gives the corresponding parallelogram area:

$$
\begin{aligned}
M&=\|\mathbf{p}\times \mathbf{q}\|\\&=\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\&=\sqrt{53}=\sqrt{53}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The requested shape is the parallelogram, so use the whole magnitude.

$$
\begin{aligned}
A&=M=\sqrt{53}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=2 t^{2}.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=0$ at $A$ and $t=1$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=4 t.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(2 \left(2 t^{2}\right)\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=4 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(2 \left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(4 t\right) \\ &=8 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{0}^{1}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{0}^{1}\left(12 t^{2}\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[4 t^{3}\right]_{0}^{1} \\ &=\left(4\right)-\left(0\right) \\ &=4
\end{aligned}
$$
